# What the program-start path of we_child.c may reach between the fork and the
# exec: every symbol that leaves wpe_child_exec's object, through every helper
# it calls.  Checked by tests/check-child-exec.sh.
#
#   symbol|mark|reason[|fact]
#
# The reason for each is that it takes no lock and allocates nothing, which is
# what a child may rely on when another thread of the editor held a lock at
# the moment of the fork (the graphical editor has one: Pango's font thread).
#
# marks:
#   SAFE     in signal-safety(7) (POSIX.1-2008 TC2 list)
#   SYSCALL  not in that list, a thin wrapper of one system call
#   EXEC     the exec call
#   RUNTIME  the compiler's or the C library's own symbol, a variable of the C
#            library (environ) included
#   DATA     a variable of the tree's own objects (we_child.c itself), read
#            from .data or .bss (none today: the child reads only its
#            arguments)
#
# The fourth field may instead be the word "optional", on a SAFE line only: the
# line permits the symbol when it is reached and is silent when it is not.  It
# is for spellings that depend on how a compiler inlines (memcpy and its
# fortified twin, open's checked twin), which no fact can follow.  A dead SAFE
# line permits nothing unsafe: it names a call that is on the list anyway.  On
# SYSCALL, EXEC, RUNTIME or DATA the word is a malformed line, and an optional
# line carries no fact.
#
# A symbol the path reaches with no line here fails the check; a line that
# nothing reaches is stale and fails it too, except where the optional fact (a
# preprocessor expression, read with the build's flags and config.h and
# -Werror=undef) is false in the build: another optimisation level, another
# compiler or another system reaches a different spelling of the same call,
# and the fact says exactly when the symbol is reached.  When the fact is
# false the symbol must not be reached; a fact that does not evaluate or is
# the literal 0 fails.  A fact
# cannot hold a vertical bar (it separates the fields): write "a or b" as
# "defined(a) + defined(b) > 0".

# --- system calls and the signal-safety(7) list ---
_exit|SAFE|ends the child without running any handler or flush
chdir|SAFE|in the list
close|SAFE|in the list
dup2|SAFE|in the list
fcntl|SAFE|in the list
fstat|SAFE|in the list
getpgrp|SAFE|in the list
getppid|SAFE|in the list
memcpy|SAFE|in the list (POSIX.1-2008 TC2): copies the prepared sentence onto the stack|optional
open|SAFE|in the list
setpgid|SAFE|in the list
setsid|SAFE|in the list
sigaction|SAFE|in the list
sigemptyset|SAFE|in the list (a macro on OpenBSD and an inline on NetBSD: no object imports it there)|optional
signal|SAFE|in the list
sigprocmask|SAFE|in the list (the child is single threaded)
stat|SAFE|in the list
tcgetpgrp|SAFE|in the list
write|SAFE|in the list

# --- thin wrappers of one system call, not in the list ---
ioctl|SYSCALL|one system call: the controlling terminal step (TIOCSCTTY)
close_range|SYSCALL|one system call, tried first and followed by a loop of close on failure|WPE_HAS_CLOSE_RANGE
closefrom|SYSCALL|one system call where the system has it and not close_range (never on glibc, where it lists /proc)|WPE_HAS_CLOSEFROM && !WPE_HAS_CLOSE_RANGE
prctl|SYSCALL|one system call: the parent-death signal and the no-new-privileges flag on Linux|(WPE_HAS_PARENT_DEATH_SIGNAL && HAVE_DECL_PR_SET_PDEATHSIG) + (WPE_HAS_NO_NEW_PRIVS && HAVE_DECL_PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS) > 0
procctl|SYSCALL|one system call: the parent-death signal and the no-new-privileges flag on FreeBSD|(WPE_HAS_PARENT_DEATH_SIGNAL && HAVE_DECL_PROC_PDEATHSIG_CTL) + (WPE_HAS_NO_NEW_PRIVS && HAVE_DECL_PROC_NO_NEW_PRIVS_CTL) > 0
setrlimit|SYSCALL|one system call: the core and file-size limits of an assistant child

# --- the exec ---
execvp|EXEC|takes no lock and allocates nothing: measured on glibc 2.43 (getenv, string functions, alloca cutoff, execve); musl and the BSDs build the path on the stack.  An assistant child is given a path with a slash (resolved in the parent), which execvp passes to execve without a search

# --- the environment of an assistant child ---
environ|RUNTIME|the C library's variable: an assistant child's environment, built in the parent, is stored there before the exec

# --- the fortified spellings of the above, and the compiler's own ---
__memcpy_chk|SAFE|memcpy with a length check against the destination's size, under _FORTIFY_SOURCE|optional
__open_2|SAFE|open with a check that no mode was needed, under _FORTIFY_SOURCE|optional
errno_location|RUNTIME|the address of the thread's errno (spelt __errno_location on glibc and musl, __error on FreeBSD and macOS, __errno on OpenBSD and NetBSD): reads thread-local storage, takes no lock
__stack_chk_fail|RUNTIME|ends the process on a smashed stack, which is what a child that cannot trust its stack must do|defined(__SSP_STRONG__) + defined(__SSP_ALL__) > 0
